#{[17592186115879 :question/worked-solution "As $x\\to\\infty$, the exponent $2x+4$ tends to $\\infty$. Since the base $2>1$,\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}2^{2x+4}=\\infty.\n$$"] [17592186115880 :question/worked-solution "Write $8^{-x}=1/8^x$. Since $8^x\\to\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty$,\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\infty}8^{-x}=0.\n$$"] [17592186115902 :question/worked-solution "Because $6>1$, $6^x\\to0$ as $x\\to-\\infty$. Multiplication by the constant $-7$ preserves this limit:\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty}(-7\\cdot6^x)=0.\n$$"] [17592186121313 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186121314] [17592186121319 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186121324] [17592186121439 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186121443] [17592186121457 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186121458] [17592186124786 :question/worked-solution "Hold $x$ constant and differentiate term by term:\n$$\nf_y=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(xy^2)+\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2\\cos y)=2xy-x^2\\sin y.\n$$"] [17592186124787 :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as constant:\n$$\nf_x=y^2\\sec^2x-\\frac{y}{x^2}.\n$$\nSince $\\sec^2\\pi=1$,\n$$\nf_x(\\pi,2)=4-\\frac2{\\pi^2}.\n$$"] [17592186124807 :question/worked-solution "The partial derivative with respect to $y$ keeps $x$ fixed and increments only $y$. Its defining difference quotient is\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(x,y+h)-f(x,y)}{h}=\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{x\\sqrt{y+h}-x\\sqrt y}{h}.\n$$\nFor the usual two-sided derivative, this limit is taken at $y>0$."] [17592186124810 :question/worked-solution "Treat $y$ as constant:\n$$\nf_x=2^x\\ln2\\tan y-\\frac{2y}{\\pi x^3}.\n$$\nAt $(1,\\pi/4)$, $\\tan(\\pi/4)=1$, so\n$$\nf_x(1,\\pi/4)=2\\ln2-\\frac{2(\\pi/4)}\\pi=2\\ln2-\\frac12.\n$$"] [17592186125867 :tutorial/content "Consider the multivariable function $f(x,y),$ given by\n\n$$\nf (x, y)= x y^2 + e^{y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to ${\\color{blue}x},$ we \"fix\" the variable $y$ (in other words, treat $y$ as if it were a constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to ${\\color{blue}x},$ as we usually would:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{\\color{blue}x}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{\\color{blue}x}}({\\color{blue}x}{y}^{2}+{e}^{y}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂{\\color{blue}x}}({\\color{blue}x})+\\frac{∂}{∂{\\color{blue}x}}({e}^{y}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot (1)+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the term $e^{y}$ vanished when taking the derivative with respect to $x.$ This is because $e^y$ is a function of $y$ only, and since we're treating $y$ as a constant, this entire term is treated as a constant and therefore differentiates to zero (just as a constant would).\n\nSimilarly, to compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to ${\\color{blue}y},$ we fix the variable $x$ and then take the derivative with respect to ${\\color{blue}y},$ as we usually would:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{\\color{blue}y}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂{\\color{blue}y}}(x{{\\color{blue}y}}^{2}+{e}^{{\\color{blue}y}}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂{\\color{blue}y}}({{\\color{blue}y}}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂{\\color{blue}y}}({e}^{{\\color{blue}y}}) \\\\  & =x\\cdot (2y)+({e}^{y}) \\\\  & =2xy+{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen we wish to evaluate a partial derivative at a point $(x_0, y_0),$ we can use any of the following notations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f(x_0,y_0)} {\\partial x} =  \\dfrac {\\partial f} {\\partial x}\\Bigg|_{(x_0,y_0)} = f_x(x_0,y_0)\n$$\n\nThe notations for $f_y(x_0,y_0)$ follow the same patterns."] [17592186144137 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186144142] [17592186144143 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186144147] [17592186147391 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + p(x)y= q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}, \\qquad q(x) = x^2, \\qquad n=-1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by $y^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\ny \\dfrac{{{\\rm{d}}y}}{{{\\rm{d}}x}} + \\dfrac{y^{2}}{x}= x^2.\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz= y^{1-n} =  y^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}  = 2y \\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\ny\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{1}{x}z  = x^2\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac{2}{x} z\n\t\t=2x^2\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}z}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)z = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{2}{x},\\,Q(x)=2{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (2/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x^2$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}z) & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (2{x}^{2}) \\\\ {x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+2xz & =2{x}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2}) & =2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 2{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{x}^{2} & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}}{5}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2{x}^{5}+C}{5{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=5C_1.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z=y^{2}.$ This results in the general solution\n\n$$\ny^{ 2} =\\dfrac{2x^5 + C}{5x^2}.\n$$\n\nFor real solutions, require $x\\ne0$ and $2x^5+C>0$. The implicit relation retains both signs $y=\\pm\\sqrt{(2x^5+C)/(5x^2)}$. The original equation is undefined at $y=0$."] [17592186147725 :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-y={y}^{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$"] [17592186147725 :question/worked-solution "Notice that our equation is an instance of the Bernoulli differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+p(x)y=q(x){y}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=-1,\\,q(x)={e}^{x},\\,n=2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we divide the differential equation by ${y}^{2},$ and we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-{y}^{-1}={e}^{x}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThen, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{1-n}={y}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-{y}^{-2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,{y}^{-2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the original equation can be written in terms of $z(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-z={e}^{x}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. But first, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z=-{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=1,\\,Q(x)=-{e}^{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z) & ={e}^{x}\\cdot (-{e}^{x}) \\\\ {e}^{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x}) & =-{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(z{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ z{e}^{x} & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2x}+{C}_{1} \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1}=\\frac{C-{e}^{2x}}{2{e}^{x}}\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{C-{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse intervals where $C-e^{2x}\\ne0$. The family displayed in the choices is nonzero. The original equation also admits $y=0$, which was excluded when dividing by a power of $y$."] [17592186147729 :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli equation has the form $y'+P(x)y=Q(x)y^n$ with $n\\ne0,1$.\n\nI has this form with $n=2$. II has a $y^2$ term instead of the required linear $P(x)y$ term. III has this form with $n=-1/2$, on its real domain $y>0$.\n\nTherefore the correct selection is **I and III only**."] [17592186147730 :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli equation has the form $y'+P(x)y=Q(x)y^n$ with $n\\ne0,1$.\n\nFor I, division by $y$ gives $y'+x^2y^2=e^xy^3$, which still has two nonlinear powers. For II, division by $y^2$ gives\n$$\ny'+x^2y=e^xy^2,\n$$\nwhich is Bernoulli with $n=2$ on intervals where $y\\ne0$. The original II also admits $y=0$, which should be checked separately when solving. III contains trigonometric functions of $y$ and does not have Bernoulli form.\n\nTherefore the correct selection is **II only**."] [17592186164402 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186164406] [17592186164407 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186164408] [17592186164411 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186164414] [17592186164416 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186164419] [17592186166551 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = \nc_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + \nc_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is unbounded, representing a spiral that is traversed counterclockwise. Thus, we must have $a \\neq 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward the origin.\n\nHence, to insure that $e^{at} \\to 0,$ we require $a < 0.$"] [17592186166552 :question/worked-solution "Let $\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i},$ where $\\mathbf{p},\\mathbf{q} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda = a + b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) = \\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{x}}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =\\mathbf{v}{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}(bt))(\\mathbf{p}+\\mathbf{q}\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{q})+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\operatorname{cos}(bt)\\mathbf{q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\textbf{x}(t) = \nc_1 e^{at}\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big) + \nc_2 e^{at}\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $\\big(\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{p}-\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ and $\\big(\\sin(bt)\\mathbf{p}+\\cos(bt)\\mathbf{q}\\big)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that $a = \\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0$ and $e^{at} = e^{0\\cdot t} = 1$ for all $t.$ Thus, we have an ellipse that curves around the origin. \n\nTherefore, since $\\text{Re}(\\lambda) = 0,$ the equilibrium point (origin) is a center."]}
